# 19. CRITICAL BUG PREVENTION — MONITOR FRONT/BACK

This is a mandatory engineering requirement.

Previous modules suffered from monitor surfaces appearing on both sides.

DO NOT REPEAT THIS BUG.

For EVERY monitor and wall display:

1. Identify the display owner/viewing position.
2. Identify the screen's actual front-facing normal.
3. Confirm front orientation.
4. Create only ONE intended display plane for each display.
5. Use an opaque rear casing.
6. Keep display plane physically in front of its bezel/back casing.
7. Do not duplicate the display texture on the rear.
8. Prevent mirrored text.
9. Check monitor stands.
10. Verify screen orientation after GLB export and reimport.

## QA DISPLAYS

Both QA monitors must face the QA chair.

## DEVOPS DISPLAYS

Both DevOps monitors must face the DevOps chair.

## WALL BOARDS

Wall displays must face their room-side viewers.

## TESTING DEVICES

Phone and tablet screens must face the intended testing viewpoint.

## NO SHORTCUTS

Do not use DoubleSide rendering as a substitute for correct geometry orientation.

Do not rotate screens toward the public camera if doing so makes them face away from their users.

Do not show a bright screen on the rear of a physical monitor.

## VALIDATION

Perform a screen-facing check in world space where feasible:

`dot(screenNormal, normalize(viewerPosition - screenPosition)) > 0.7`

Use actual transformed vectors and correct local front axes.

This test should not blindly substitute for visual inspection.

Inspect each display from front, side, rear, and user seating position.

